# The MIPLIB 2017 benchmark set: all 240 instances of benchmark-v2.test,
# pinned (02-491). It is the set Mittelmann's MILP benchmark times, and
# bench/miplib2017.manifest is 30 of its rows, identical here. Not a gate
# set and not for bench/run: the 7 infeasible rows carry 0 and would read
# as optima there. bench/measurements/02-491/run.sh reads it, times each
# instance and scores it by the MIPLIB checker's rule.
#
#   bench/fetch.sh -m bench/miplib2017-bench.manifest \
#       -b https://miplib.zib.de/WebData/instances -p mps-gz \
#       bench/instances-miplib2017-bench
#
# 639 MB of .mps.gz, expanded to 7.3 GB of the .mps the CLI reads.
#
# Optima are miplib2017-v31.solu's: 232 =opt=, 1 =best= (supportcase22,
# source `best`, not proven) and 7 =inf= (source `infeasible`, optimum 0).
# The current file, v37, agrees on 238. On two it lists lower values that
# v36 took as objective improvements below the earlier optimum: binkar10_1
# 6741.38002393972 and neos-3754480-nidda 12939.7540104743. Floating-point
# solvers stop at v31's values, and Mittelmann's table does too (HiGHS
# 6742.20002 on binkar10_1, marked ok). Rows and columns are what
# `jaos stats` reads, the constraints and variables of
# miplib.zib.de/tag_benchmark.html but on two: mad and neos-933966 carry a
# second N row, which JAOS keeps as a free row and the site does not count.
# JAOS refuses leo1 and leo2 (an FR bound with a value), and there the
# columns are the site's. No file has an objective constant or OBJSENSE,
# so objconst is 0 throughout.
#
# name sha256 rows cols optimum source objconst
